@@ -0,0 +1,131 @@
+# Design
+
+## Context
+
+See proposal.md for motivation. Observed current state (all verified in this checkout):
+
+- A recognised retry of any mutation reaches one shared path: `run_idempotent_mutation_inner`
+  classifies the request via `classify_existing_keys`; `KeyState::Complete(original_decision_sequence)`
+  flows to `reply_with_current_status`, which renders `execution::status::execute` and lets
+  `annotate_recognized_retry` insert `data.idempotency = {recognizedRetry: true,
+  originalDecisionSequence}` (`apps/cli/src/commands/serve/mod.rs`). There are exactly two call
+  sites producing `Complete`: the initial pre-flight and the post-append re-classification after a
+  `GHE003_IDEMPOTENCY_CONFLICT` race loser. Both must get the new value for free.
+- Classification is already identity-exact: `KeyPresence::Present` requires the committed key to
+  equal the derived key **and** `ExpectedDecision::matches(event)`, which for the signal command
+  compares actor, scope/stream, execution, event kind, and the signal payload fields including
+  `payload.signal_id == request body signal.id`. A `Present` match for `execution.signal` is
+  therefore guaranteed to be the `signal_recorded` event carrying the exact durable `signalId` the
+  first reply returned.
+- The fresh success shape comes from `execution::signal::execute`, which puts `signalId` at
+  `data.signalId`. The status rendering (`render_read`) has no top-level `signalId` key today, so
+  inserting one shadows nothing.
+- An in-process race test (`mod.rs`, two-attempt signal) asserts **exact equality of the
+  `data.idempotency` sub-object**; `apps/cli/tests/api_http.rs` already contains the three tests
+  named in the proposal (recognised retry, mismatched actor, wrong-kind event under the same key).
+- No `openspec/specs/` capability existed before this change; `execution-signal` is introduced here.
+
+## Goals / Non-Goals
+
+**Goals:**
+
+- A recognised retry of `execution.signal` returns the original durable `signalId`, sourced from
+  the matched committed event (never re-derived from the request).
+- Both `Complete` call sites (pre-flight and post-append re-classification) behave identically.
+- Refusal paths (mismatched actor, wrong kind → `Divergent`; partial → `Partial`) keep their
+  current shape and gain no `signalId`.
+
+**Non-Goals:**
+
+- No `signalId` on recognised retries of any other mutation endpoint.
+- No change to fresh signal replies, to the `idempotency` proof object's own contents, to other
+  refusal bodies, or to any core crate, schema, or CLI command surface.
+- No change to how `Divergent`/`Partial` classify (no new matching rules).
+
+## Decisions
+
+### D1: Place `signalId` at `data.signalId`, mirroring the fresh reply
+
+A recognised-retry reply's `data` gains a top-level `signalId` next to (not inside) the
+`idempotency` object.
+
+- Why: a client correlating "my lost-ACK retry" with the original reads `reply.data.signalId`
+  identically whether or not the retry was recognised. Putting it inside `idempotency` would make
+  the two lookups asymmetric and would break the in-process race test's exact-equality assertion on
+  the `idempotency` sub-object.
+- Alternative considered: `data.idempotency.signalId`. Rejected for the two reasons above; it would
+  also blur the distinction between "proof this is a retry" and "identity of the retried record".
+- Alternative considered: post-processing in the route handler (`routes.rs`). Rejected: the route
+  does not know whether classification recognised the retry, so it would have to re-read the
+  classification result or the stream.
+
+### D2: Carry the value out of classification; do not re-read the stream for it
+
+Extend the recognised-retry data flow so the matched event's durable `signalId` travels with
+`KeyState::Complete` to `reply_with_current_status`:
+
+- In `classify_one_key`/`classify_existing_keys`, when a key classifies `Present` and its matched
+  envelope is `EventKind::SignalRecorded(payload)`, record `payload.signal_id` alongside the
+  recorded sequence — specifically for the event whose sequence becomes `original_decision_sequence`
+  (for the signal command that is the single matched decision event; the rule is stated generally
+  so multi-key commands stay correct by construction, not by assumption).
+- `reply_with_current_status` / `annotate_recognized_retry` insert `data.signalId` iff that value
+  was captured. No re-open of the store, no second identity check: the `matches()` gate that made
+  the event `Present` is the single authority for both the recognition and the extracted value.
+
+- Alternative considered: re-locate the event at `originalDecisionSequence` inside the reply
+  function and validate it again. Rejected: second read of an untrusted repository file, second
+  place to express the kind/identity gate, and the value could disagree with what classification
+  actually authenticated if the read raced (it cannot today, since the pre-flight already returned
+  before any append, but the re-validation would duplicate rules rather than share them).
+- Alternative considered: compute `signalId` from the request body. Rejected: the durable record is
+  the source of truth; the body is caller-owned input that `matches()` merely *compared* against the
+  committed payload.
+
+### D3: Failure and non-recognition behavior is untouched
+
+- The existing 500 path when the status value cannot carry the proof (`IDEMPOTENCY_REPLY_CODE`)
+  behaves exactly as today.
+- `Divergent` (mismatched actor, wrong-kind event, divergent digest) and `Partial` responses keep
+  their current bodies: `GHE003_IDEMPOTENCY_CONFLICT`, `data.currentHead`, no `idempotency`, and —
+  because they never reach `annotate_recognized_retry` — no `signalId`.
+
+## Risks / Trade-offs
+
+- [Additive wire field read by unknown future clients] → Field is purely additive JSON on one
+  success path; consumers ignoring unknown keys are unaffected. The new capability spec pins that
+  no other endpoint's path gains `signalId`.
+- [Existing assertions freeze current shapes] → The in-process race test asserts the
+  `idempotency` sub-object exactly (unaffected by a top-level `data` field); the `api_http.rs`
+  retry tests assert selected fields, and this change extends them rather than rewriting them.
+  Residual risk noted: if any other golden-style comparison of the full retry body exists
+  elsewhere in the suite, the reached tests will surface it during verification — verification runs
+  the whole `api_http` suite plus the `serve` module unit tests.
+- [Value extracted from untrusted repository bytes] → Extraction happens only after the same
+  `matches()` identity gate that authenticates the retry; a wrong-kind or mismatched-actor event
+  classifies `Divergent` instead of `Present` and is never read for a `signalId`.
+- [Rollback] → Single-purpose change in two files; reverting the implementation commit removes the
+  field from the wire with no state migration (nothing is persisted differently).
+
+## Migration Plan
+
+1. Implement D2 in `apps/cli/src/commands/serve/mod.rs`; extend the three `api_http.rs` tests per
+   the proposal (positive assertion first to observe RED, then implement to GREEN).
+2. Verification evidence: the extended `api_http` tests red without the code change and green with
+   it, plus the existing serve-layer unit tests staying green.
+3. No deploy order, no data migration; rollback = revert.
+
+## Open Questions
+
+None. Placement (D1) and sourcing (D2) were resolved here; both were required before tasks could be
+broken down, so nothing deferrable remains.
+
+**Recorded assumption:** the change name `bench-feature` was supplied by the task and is used
+verbatim although the work concerns the signal retry reply; the name carries no semantic weight in
+this artifact set.
+
+**Recorded assumption (apply):** the lib-side verification commands are written as
+`cargo +1.97.1 test -p graphhelm-cli --lib --locked …`, but `graphhelm-cli` declares no library
+target (a bin named `graphhelm` only) — the crate's unit tests live under the binary target. Every
+`--lib` invocation above was therefore run as `--bin graphhelm`; the test names, filters, and pass
+criteria are otherwise exactly as written.
